{
  "text": " So this is very interesting. I love this question because a lot of people are optimistic because it's a more traditional killed protein type vaccine. It's not a gene based vaccine that again these are all in trial on humanity. And I know Steve wants to talk to me about community and the reason we can't use it in the US, because nobody can slew if it's not here. But anyway, Novavax, it's a killed protein. It's generated in moth cells and culture, which is interesting. But you have to think it is back at history. We've never been able to vaccinate against the coronavirus. This family of viruses for a very good reason, because they have a slow mutational drift constantly. Same reason we don't have a vaccine against the common cold. Same reason, 40 years later we don't have an HIV vaccine. The spike on the HIV virus is similar, different, but same concept. They mutationally drift over time. So if you look at SARS-CoV-1, they use dead proteins in those studies in the animal models and it was a dead spike. I mean, it's an inert protein. It's not an active protein. You developed antibodies in an immune response against it. But when reexposed to a wild type variant of the virus a couple months later, now you got a hyperimmune reaction to it. These never really made it into humans because that's called antibody dependent enhancement. You recognize one pattern just enough to wear down the road. And antibody is forever. A good one is great. A bad one is not a good idea. And so that protein on SARS-CoV-1 drifted just enough that in the animal models they were hyperimmune down the road. Now think about Novavax. You have an inert protein. It's still a spike model. What did we look from those other things? The spike is toxic. You're not generating it through your own body. Now your body's not making it. But your immune system is going to remember this spike and you're going to have an antibody. Great. Until along comes mu or psi or zeta and the shape is different. Now those antibodies you developed from this spike protein, instead of binding and neutralizing and clearing and mopping up virus, down the road they bind. They don't neutralize. And when you have binding, not neutralizing antibodies, now they can trudge and horse the virus into your immune cells, overtake your immune system. And actually you get worse disease faster and a higher risk of death. And so the Novavax is cool in concept if this were another family of viruses. This is not. This is a coronavirus family. We know the history of coronaviruses. I'm not saying everybody's going to have an adverse reaction, but if you want to look up a really good story, look up the history of dengue vaccines in the Philippines. And that's a canary in the coal mine for us with these vaccines and with this family of viruses. With the dengue vaccine there are now hundreds of thousands of children that got that dengue vaccine that are primed for the rest of their life for every dengue virus season that comes along for a more severe reaction, a higher risk of hospitalization and a higher risk of death. They've been primed with an antibody that at first seems great but down the road becomes your enemy. So the moral is be careful what you ask for. Novavax will have a better safety profile than the mRNA vaccines, but there's still the problem of original antigenic sin. There's still the problem, you know, of, of, you know, vaccine enhanced replication and infectivity basically, those don't go away. That can be the, you know, that's the deciding factor.",
